        <p class="article__lead reveal">In the far north west of Rwanda, five great volcanoes rise into the cloud, and on their bamboo clad slopes live the mountain gorillas that made this park world famous. The Volcanoes National Park is the most luxurious and accessible place on earth to meet them, just a couple of hours from Kigali.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Why visit the Volcanoes National Park?</h2>
        <p class="reveal">This is the Rwandan section of the Virunga Massif, a chain of volcanoes shared with Uganda and the DR Congo. It was Dian Fossey's home and research base, and remains a conservation success story: gorilla numbers are rising, and Rwanda celebrates each new birth at the annual Kwita Izina naming ceremony. Short transfers and exceptional lodges make it a refined, easy adventure.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Gorilla trekking</h2>
        <p class="reveal">After an early briefing with traditional dancing at park headquarters, you set out with a ranger and trackers. The hike through farmland and into the bamboo can take one to four hours, and then you have a regulated, unforgettable hour with a habituated gorilla family. It is moving beyond words, and over far too soon.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Golden monkeys &amp; more</h2>
        <ul class="reveal">
          <li><strong>Golden monkeys:</strong> a separate trek to find troops of these playful, endangered, bamboo loving primates.</li>
          <li><strong>Dian Fossey hike:</strong> a moderately tough walk to the Karisoke research camp and her tomb.</li>
          <li><strong>Volcano climbs &amp; lakes:</strong> the Bisoke crater lake, the Musanze caves and the scenic twin lakes of Burera and Ruhondo.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="reveal">Permits &amp; how hard it is</h2>
        <p class="reveal">A Rwanda gorilla permit is premium priced and strictly limited; we book yours well in advance. Trekking suits reasonably fit travellers aged 15 and over, and local porters are available, and warmly recommended, to carry your daypack and help on the slopes. There is no rush; you go at your own pace.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Best time to visit</h2>
        <p class="reveal">Gorilla permits are available year round, but trekking is easiest in the drier months, <strong>June to September</strong> and <strong>December to February</strong>, when the trails are firmest. The green seasons are lush, atmospheric and quieter. See our <a href="best-time-rwanda.html">best time to visit Rwanda</a> guide for more.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Where to stay &amp; how to get there</h2>
        <p class="reveal">The park is ringed by some of Africa's finest lodges, from reforested forest domes to elegant suites on the park's edge and warm community owned lodges. Most guests drive the smooth two to three hours from Kigali, with the terraced hills unrolling the whole way. We arrange every transfer and the permit.</p>

